+      "counter_evidence": [
+        {
+          "type": "guard",
+          "file": "inc/class-destination-s3.php",
+          "line": 57,
+          "detail": "The legacy S3 AJAX editor is not open to the read-only role: `edit_ajax()` aborts unless the caller has `backwpup_jobs_edit` and a valid `backwpup_ajax_nonce`."
+        },
+        {
+          "type": "guard",
+          "file": "src/Backups/API/Rest.php",
+          "line": 229,
+          "detail": "The backup start path only proceeds when at least one configured destination passes `can_run()`, so the REST capability mismatch alone does not prove that arbitrary S3 values immediately produce a successful upload."
+        },
+        {
+          "type": "guard",
+          "file": "inc/class-destination-s3.php",
+          "line": 741,
+          "detail": "For S3, `can_run()` only returns true when stored credentials include both `s3accesskey` and `s3secretkey`; the code review therefore shows state change, but not verified bucket ownership or successful object creation."
+        }
+      ],
+      "missing_evidence": [
+        "Need a captured low-privilege run showing that the same `backwpup_check` session can call `/wp-json/backwpup/v1/cloudsaveandtest`, `/wp-json/backwpup/v1/updatejob`, and `/wp-json/backwpup/v1/startbackup` with a valid `X-WP-Nonce` and an existing file-producing job ID.",
+        "Need end-to-end proof that the chosen job actually produces a backup file and that `BackWPup_Destination_S3::job_run_archive()` uploads that archive into the attacker-controlled bucket, rather than only storing credentials in `backwpup_jobs`.",
+        "Need request-shape confirmation that the exploit uses form-encoded parameters, because `BackWPup_Destination_S3::edit_form_post_save()` consumes `$_POST` at `inc/class-destination-s3.php:163-243` instead of reading the `WP_REST_Request` object directly."
+      ],
+      "dynamic_test_plan": [
+        "Step 1: Create at least one existing BackWPup job that produces a file archive, create a tester-controlled S3 bucket, and assign a second user the shipped `backwpup_check` role.",
+        "Step 2: Log in as the `backwpup_check` user, open a BackWPup admin page, capture `backwpupApi.nonce`, and obtain a real job ID from the job-card markup exposed under the broad `backwpup` permission, for example the `data-job-id` attributes rendered in `components/job-item.php:86-92` and `components/job-item.php:150-154`.",
+        "Step 3: Send a form-encoded `POST /wp-json/backwpup/v1/cloudsaveandtest` with header `X-WP-Nonce: <nonce>` and body fields such as `cloud_name=s3`, `job_id=<jobid>`, `s3accesskey=<attacker key>`, `s3secretkey=<attacker secret>`, `s3bucket=<attacker bucket>`, `s3region=<region>`, and `s3dir=loot/`; then verify in the database or UI that the target job's S3 settings inside site option `backwpup_jobs` changed.",
+        "Step 4: Send a form-encoded `POST /wp-json/backwpup/v1/updatejob` with `job_id=<jobid>` and `storage_destinations[]` containing the existing destinations plus `S3`, then send `POST /wp-json/backwpup/v1/startbackup` with the same `job_id` and the captured `X-WP-Nonce`.",
+        "Step 5: Verify measurable impact by confirming a new archive appears in the attacker bucket and, if needed, by checking the BackWPup log for the `Backup transferred to ...` message emitted from `inc/class-destination-s3.php:679-685`."
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "finding_id": "BWPUP-P2-002",
+      "vuln_type": "Broken Access Control",
+      "minimum_role_capability": "plugin-defined read-only role backwpup_check",
+      "status_transition": "UNCERTAIN -> keep",
+      "original_status": "UNCERTAIN",
+      "verdict_change": "keep",
+      "suggested_field_corrections": {
+        "entry_point_id": "ep_rest_backwpup_v1__process_bulk_actions",
+        "source_file_path": "src/Backups/API/Rest.php",
+        "source_line_number": 360,
+        "sink_file_path": "inc/class-destination-folder.php",
+        "sink_line_number": 62
+      },
+      "reason": "The REST bulk-delete route and local-folder delete sink are both real, and the Phase 3a mismatch is again a sink-location correction because `process_bulk_actions()` starts at `src/Backups/API/Rest.php:360` while `BackWPup_Destination_Folder::file_delete()` starts at `inc/class-destination-folder.php:62`. I recommend keeping the finding `UNCERTAIN` because the ordinary backups UI does withhold a ready-made delete payload from users lacking `backwpup_backups_delete`, but the same low-privileged user still appears able to derive the needed `job_id`, destination, and filename from backups-list HTML returned under the broader `backwpup` permission.",
+      "counter_evidence": [
+        {
+          "type": "guard",
+          "file": "inc/Utils/BackWPupHelpers.php",
+          "line": 197,
+          "detail": "The new backups UI does not directly hand `backwpup_check` a delete URL: `dataset-delete` is only populated when the current user already has `backwpup_backups_delete`."
+        },
+        {
+          "type": "unreachable",
+          "file": "components/table-row-backups.php",
+          "line": 54,
+          "detail": "The checkbox payload comes from `json_encode( $backup['dataset-delete'] ?? null )`, so a stock bulk-delete click from a non-deleter should submit `null` and then fail the REST handler's `data-url` requirement instead of deleting immediately."
+        },
+        {
